{"repositoryId":1423025090,"availability":"available","full":"jonclegg/roadster-physics-check","name":"roadster-physics-check","description":"Each stunt from Tesla's Oct 2026 Roadster reveal, rebuilt as a browser physics simulation. Change the hardware and watch it crash.","category":"Design","language":"JavaScript","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-10-17T06:30:44Z","updated":"2026-10-18T17:18:46Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://wooten-labs.com/roadster/","topics":["cold-gas-thrusters","physics-simulation","rigid-body","tesla","threejs"],"demo":"https://wooten-labs.com/roadster/","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-18T18:14:48.656Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-18T18:14:48.656Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-18T18:14:48.656Z","lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-18T18:14:48.656Z","discoveredVia":[],"metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-18T18:14:48.656Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Three.js"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Could Tesla's flying Roadster actually do those stunts on 600 litres of compressed air?","Each stunt from Tesla's October 15, 2026 Roadster reveal, rebuilt as a physics simulation that runs in your browser. We read the on-screen telemetry off Tesla's stream frame by frame. Each stunt became a program for a simple flight computer, and the car flies that program as a rigid body with 10 thrusters, a real-gas nitrogen tank, fan suction and tyre grip. Every assumption is a dial. Get the hardware wrong and the car crashes.","The kickflip at our best-estimate hardware. It clears the parked cars and lands at 0.6 m/s with 27% of its usable air to spare. The gold dots on the charts are the broadcast's own gauge readings."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/jonclegg/roadster-physics-check/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"1aec2da51f8891487b7cdf7caacf8ec628743d1bc37d0a91410e78d396923f5b","extractedAt":"2026-10-18T18:14:48.656Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-18T18:14:48.656Z","projectInsights":